# Incident 004 — Resource Pressure

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Incident** | Resource pressure — induced CPU/memory stress |
| **Date** | [fill in run date] |
| **Severity** | Medium — degraded performance, contained by resource limits |
| **Symptoms** | High CPU/memory usage reported in Grafana; possible pod restarts |
| **Impact** | Increased latency on the affected pod; other pods unaffected due to per-pod resource limits |
| **Detection** | Grafana CPU/memory panel spike; Datadog resource utilization monitor |
| **Investigation** | `kubectl top pods` showed usage approaching the configured limit; `kubectl describe pod` checked for Throttling/OOMKilled events |
| **Root Cause** | Controlled stress workload (`stress --cpu 2 --vm 1`) run inside the namespace to simulate load |
| **Resolution** | Stress pod removed; additional replica added to spread load |
| **Verification** | CPU/memory returned to baseline in Grafana; no further restarts observed |
| **Preventive Action** | Horizontal Pod Autoscaler planned so replica count responds automatically to sustained load |
| **Lessons Learned** | Resource requests/limits correctly contained the impact to a single pod, confirming the hardening applied to workloads works as intended |

---

## Reproduction steps

```bash
# Induce controlled CPU/memory pressure
kubectl run stress-test --image=polinux/stress -n employee-management-app --restart=Never -- \
  stress --cpu 2 --vm 1 --vm-bytes 256M --timeout 120s

# Observe
kubectl top pods -n employee-management-app
kubectl describe pod employee-app-<id> -n employee-management-app   # check for OOMKilled / Throttling
kubectl get events -n employee-management-app --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

# Recovery action
kubectl scale deployment employee-app -n employee-management-app --replicas=3
kubectl delete pod stress-test -n employee-management-app
```

